# Copy to .env and fill in — or run ./scripts/init-env.sh, which copies this file and auto-fills the
# two locally-minted secrets (FSM_TOKEN_KEY, SESSION_SECRET) for you. Each key is tagged in its
# comment:
#   REQUIRED — set this or the app can't do what it's for. A few let the process start while blank,
#              but then no one can sign in, or bookings never reach a real Google calendar — so they
#              are mandatory for the product, not just for boot.
#   AUTO     — filled by scripts/init-env.sh; set it by hand only if you create .env yourself.
#   OPTIONAL — a blank value disables only that one feature (holidays, email) while the rest of the
#              app keeps working; a key already showing a value falls back to that default.

# ── Database & environment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED — Postgres connection; this default matches the bundled docker-compose database.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://fsm:fsm@localhost:5432/fsm
# OPTIONAL — deployment environment: local | test | staging | prod (default: local).
APP_ENV=local
# OPTIONAL — IANA timezone the service region operates in.
TIMEZONE=Asia/Jerusalem

# OPTIONAL — root log level for every backend service. Set INFO or DEBUG to watch normal flow;
# successful /health and /ready probe lines are demoted to DEBUG so INFO stays readable.
FSM_LOG_LEVEL=WARNING
# OPTIONAL — per-module levels layered over FSM_LOG_LEVEL, comma-separated,
# e.g. fsm.google_calendar=DEBUG,fsm.platform.sync_runner=INFO
FSM_LOG_LEVELS=

# ── Locally-minted secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two are minted by scripts/init-env.sh, so you normally never touch them. They are required and
# always present; generate them by hand only if you build .env without that script.
# SESSION_SECRET — signs the session cookie the whole sign-in flow depends on. Blank means no session
#   middleware is mounted, so even a fully configured Google sign-in fails.
#     ./scripts/generate-secret.sh session-secret
SESSION_SECRET=
# FSM_TOKEN_KEY — encrypts stored technician refresh tokens at rest, so it gates technician calendar
#   connect (see the Google section). No default is shipped on purpose — a key committed to the repo
#   would let anyone decrypt every deployment's tokens. Keep it stable: rotating it makes
#   already-stored calendar connections undecryptable.
#     ./scripts/generate-secret.sh token-key
FSM_TOKEN_KEY=

# ── Google sign-in & technician calendar (REQUIRED) ──────────────────────────────
# A single OAuth web client powers the two halves of the core flow. They are two separate Google
# consents that share this one client — different scopes and different redirect URIs (below) — so
# set these once to enable them:
#   - Sign-in: Google OIDC is the only sign-in path, and every booking and scheduling route needs a
#     signed-in session. Requests the openid/email/profile scopes; blank means no one can sign in and
#     nothing works.
#   - Technician calendar: a second consent in which a technician connects their Google Calendar (the
#     same client, plus the auto-minted FSM_TOKEN_KEY above). The app then reads their free/busy when
#     offering slots and projects confirmed bookings onto that calendar. This calendar connection is
#     the core of the slice, not an add-on — self-booking "on a real Google calendar" is the goal.
# With the client unset the server still starts, but no one can sign in — and every booking and
# scheduling route requires a signed-in session, so nothing works until this is configured. Google
# sign-in is mandatory, not a degraded mode you can skip.
#
# Calendar scope: the calendar connect flow requests two narrow scopes by privacy-by-construction
# design — calendar.app.created (confines the app to a dedicated "Field Service" calendar it creates,
# so the technician's private calendars stay invisible) and calendar.freebusy (opaque busy/free
# intervals for slot reads, never event titles or attendees). The broad
# https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar scope is deliberately never requested. The granted scopes
# are defined in google_calendar/scopes.py.
#
# Where to get GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET — one-time setup at
# https://console.cloud.google.com :
#   1. Create or select a project.
#   2. APIs & Services > Library > enable "Google Calendar API" (the technician calendar uses it).
#   3. Configure the OAuth consent screen. In the current console this lives under "Google Auth
#      Platform" (formerly "OAuth consent screen"); on a fresh project click "Get started" on its
#      Overview, which walks you through the first two sections. Its settings are split across:
#        - Branding: app name, support email, and links shown on the consent screen.
#        - Audience: set User type to External, and while the app is unverified add your own Google
#          account under "Test users" so you can sign in.
#        - Data Access: click "Add or remove scopes". In the panel, filter by "calendar" and tick
#          .../auth/calendar.app.created and .../auth/calendar.freebusy; leave the broad
#          .../auth/calendar unticked (remove it if present). Also tick the basic openid,
#          .../auth/userinfo.email, and .../auth/userinfo.profile scopes for sign-in. Click Update,
#          then Save. Only after saving do the scopes appear on this page — all of them under "Your
#          non-sensitive scopes", since the narrow calendar scopes are not sensitive.
#      Verification depends on Publishing status (under Audience):
#        - Testing (default — keep it for dev): you skip verification. Add yourself (and up to 100
#          others) as test users. At runtime each test user will hit a "Google hasn't verified this
#          app" screen and will have to proceed via Advanced > Go to <app> to finish connecting.
#        - Production: to open the app to any Google account, publish it and pass Google's review
#          (Verification Center) if it ever requests sensitive scopes. Once verified, users will no
#          longer see that warning.
#   4. Google Auth Platform > Clients > Create client > "Web application". Under "Authorized redirect
#      URIs" add these four callbacks, verbatim — one sign-in URI per role port plus the technician
#      calendar-connect URI (this replaces the old APIs & Services > Credentials path):
#        http://localhost:8001/auth/google/callback
#        http://localhost:8002/auth/google/callback
#        http://localhost:8003/auth/google/callback
#        http://localhost:8001/calendar/connect/callback
#      Google accepts a loopback redirect URI only as bare localhost/127.0.0.1 with a port, not a
#      *.localhost subdomain — so each role signs in on its own localhost port. This works the same in
#      either run mode (docker default and --host): start with ./scripts/start.sh and sign in at
#      http://localhost:8003 for the back office, :8001 technician, :8002 customer. A production
#      deployment fronts the roles by hostname over https and registers those https://... callbacks.
#   5. Copy the generated Client ID and Client secret into the two keys here.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OPTIONAL — leave blank (default) to derive the callback per request from the host the flow began on,
# so each role completes OAuth on its own edge host above. Set an explicit value only for a fixed
# single public URL, and register exactly that value on the OAuth client.
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=
GOOGLE_CALENDAR_REDIRECT_URI=

# ── Background calendar workers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Calendar sync is ON in every scripted launch. scripts/start.sh and docker-compose force the dispatch
# and sync flags true on the backoffice process, and backoffice is part of the default role set — so a standard
# deployment runs the full two-way Google sync without touching these keys. The two halves are:
#   - dispatch (FSM_DISPATCH_ENABLED): outbound — drain the outbox, pushing confirmed bookings onto
#     the technician's Google calendar.
#   - sync (FSM_SYNC_ENABLED): inbound — poll Google for technician-side edits and reconcile them
#     back into the FSM database.
# The false values below are per-process guards, not a "feature off" switch: exactly one process may
# own the workers, or several would drain the shared outbox and poll Google redundantly. Backoffice
# is that owner; technician/customer must keep these off. Set them by hand only if you bypass the
# launch scripts (e.g. run uvicorn directly) or deploy without the backoffice role — in which case
# nothing reaches a real calendar until you turn dispatch on somewhere.
# Outbound: push confirmed bookings onto technician calendars.
FSM_DISPATCH_ENABLED=false
FSM_DISPATCH_INTERVAL_SECONDS=5.0
# TECHNICIAN_APP_URL — deliberately not a key in this file. It is the public base URL of the
# technician-facing deployment, from which the dispatcher builds the photo links it writes into
# calendar events; a dispatch-enabled process refuses to start without it. Both scripted launches
# supply http://localhost:8001 (the technician edge) alongside turning dispatch on; export
# TECHNICIAN_APP_URL before launching to point the links at a public deployment instead. A value
# in this file would be shadowed by the launchers' environment, which is why none is offered here.
# Inbound: poll Google for technician-side edits and reconcile them into the FSM database.
FSM_SYNC_ENABLED=false
FSM_SYNC_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30.0

# ── Booking churn rate limit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — a customer who cancels FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_LIMIT or more appointments within the last
# FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_WINDOW_HOURS is blocked from booking until FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_COOLOFF_HOURS
# elapse after their latest cancellation; the rejection states when booking reopens.
# FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_LIMIT=0 disables the check.
FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_LIMIT=3
FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_WINDOW_HOURS=24.0
FSM_BOOKING_CANCEL_COOLOFF_HOURS=24.0

# ── Public holidays (OPTIONAL feature) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Set GOOGLE_API_KEY + HOLIDAY_CALENDAR_ID to exclude public holidays from availability;
# blank means availability ignores holidays.
#
# GOOGLE_API_KEY is a Google Cloud *API key* — a different credential from the OAuth Client ID/secret
# above — used to read a public calendar with no per-user OAuth grant. One-time setup at
# https://console.cloud.google.com, in the same project as the OAuth client:
#   1. APIs & Services > Library > "Google Calendar API" enabled (already done for the OAuth client).
#   2. APIs & Services > Credentials > Create credentials > API key.
#   3. Restrict the key to the Google Calendar API (recommended), then copy it here.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# Public holiday calendar id, e.g. en.usa#holiday@group.v.calendar.google.com
HOLIDAY_CALENDAR_ID=
# OPTIONAL — how many years ahead to cache holidays (default: 1).
HOLIDAY_REFRESH_YEARS_AHEAD=1

# ── Email / .ics delivery (OPTIONAL feature) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Set SMTP_HOST + SMTP_USER to email booking notifications (with an .ics invite); blank keeps
# notifications in the in-app feed only. SMTP_USER is the account we authenticate as, and invites
# are sent from that same address. For Gmail, SMTP_PASS is a 16-character App Password (not your
# account password; requires 2-Step Verification). This is the only external integration not Google.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
# OPTIONAL — SMTP_FROM overrides the sender; blank sends from SMTP_USER (the default), set it only
# when the From address must differ from the login. SMTP_PORT and SMTP_USE_TLS show their defaults.
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USE_TLS=true

# ── Back office & live updates ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_EMAILS: comma-separated emails granted the ADMIN (back-office) role on first sign-in via the
# back-office host. This is the only path to ADMIN, so the people who run the agency go here. Blank
# means no one can reach the back office. Takes effect only with Google sign-in configured, since the
# role is granted during sign-in.
ADMIN_EMAILS=
# REDIS_URL: pub/sub broker carrying live SSE events (technician approval queue, approval results)
# between the per-role processes. scripts/start.sh defaults it to the bundled Redis; blank falls back
# to an in-process bus that only delivers within a single process (no cross-host live updates).
REDIS_URL=

# ── AI knowledge base & triage assistant (OPTIONAL feature) ──────────────────────
# Configuration for the assist context, which has two independently gated features:
#   - The back-office knowledge base — admins upload pdf/md/txt documents, which are chunked,
#     embedded, and searchable from the admin panel. Gated by ASSIST_EMBEDDINGS.
#   - The customer triage chat — the assistant works the problem with the customer and opens a
#     service call with a summary when it runs out of safe suggestions. Gated by ASSIST_MODEL.
#     With it unset the customer page shows the plain description form instead.
#
# Key dependency rule — each configured provider must have its key:
#   - ASSIST_EMBEDDINGS plus the key of its provider enables the knowledge base. Embeddings are
#     OpenAI-only (Anthropic offers no embeddings API), so with the value below OPENAI_API_KEY
#     enables it.
#   - ASSIST_MODEL plus the key of its provider enables the triage chat.
# Leaving either model or its key blank disables that feature; the code has no fallback model —
# this file is where the models live.
# OPTIONAL — provider:model selecting the chat model (Anthropic or OpenAI). Switching providers
# needs no code change; only this value and the matching key below.
ASSIST_MODEL=anthropic:claude-sonnet-5
# OPTIONAL — provider:model selecting the embedding model. Changing it invalidates the
# knowledge-base index: vectors from different models are not comparable, so every document must be
# re-embedded (re-index action in the back-office panel) before search works again.
ASSIST_EMBEDDINGS=openai:text-embedding-3-small
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── Customer photo storage (rides the triage chat) ───────────────────────────────
# Object storage for photos the customer attaches in the triage chat: the full-resolution
# original is kept for the technician, the model receives a downscaled copy. The defaults match
# the bundled docker-compose MinIO service, like DATABASE_URL does for Postgres — override them
# only to point at an external S3-compatible store. Ignored while the triage chat is disabled.
MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost:9100
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=fsm
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=fsm-minio-local
MINIO_BUCKET=fsm-photos
MINIO_SECURE=false

# ── Operational (set automatically by scripts/start.sh) ──────────────────────────
# Override only if you run uvicorn directly.
# FSM_ROLE: which app this process serves — technician | customer | backoffice.
# FSM_FRONTEND_DIST: absolute path to the built React app (frontend/dist) served at "/".
